# Vantage Movers Server Organization

- `vantage-main-server` is the Vercel-hosted Express REST API for Vantage Movers. Production URL: `https://vantage-movers-main-server.vercel.app`.
- Runtime API code belongs under `api/`. Operational/personal tooling belongs under `scripts/`. Architecture, schema, workflow, and migration notes belong under `docs/`.
- Before changing write paths, identify `TEST_MODE`, selected Mongo DB, target Google Sheet env vars, and whether the code can touch CRM, Postman, or production customer data.

## API Launch Map

- Start request tracing at `api/index.ts`: Express app setup, health/db probes, logging, CORS/body parsing, and route mounting.
- `api/routes/` owns HTTP boundaries. Keep routes thin: authenticate, parse with Zod, call services, format responses, and avoid business logic.
- `api/validation/` owns request/operation schemas. `api/validation/v1.validation.ts` is the public v1 barrel; detailed schemas live in `api/validation/v1/`.
- `api/services/` owns business behavior and side effects. Use the domain folders first: `leads/`, `bookings/`, `cancellations/`, `customers/`, `admin/`, `ringcentral/`, `googleSheets/`, `crm/`, `reconciliation/`, `enrichment/`, `search/`, and `errors/`. Legacy barrels such as `v1.service.ts`, `crm.service.ts`, and search/reconciliation wrappers may preserve API compatibility.
- `api/models/` owns runtime Mongoose schemas and shared schema helpers. Mongo/API fields should stay `snake_case`; only Google Sheets projections should produce owner-facing Title Case columns.
- `api/config/` owns mode-aware domain configuration. Add source companies, sheet tabs, headers, CPL rules, Mongo database names, and Google auth env selection here instead of scattering `process.env` reads.
- `api/middleware/` owns request guards and request-level behavior such as API secret enforcement and HTTP logging.
- `api/utils/` owns small shared helpers, currently including logging sanitizers, Google Sheets range helpers, location normalization, and CSV serialization.
- `api/db.ts` is the shared Mongo connection helper. Runtime code should reuse it and preserve the `TEST_MODE` database boundary.

## RingCentral Service Map

- `api/routes/ringcentral-webhook.routes.ts`, `ringcentral-webhook-local.routes.ts`, and `ringcentral-cron.routes.ts` are HTTP/cron boundaries only; keep qualification, persistence, and lead-write behavior in services.
- `api/services/ringcentral/` owns RingCentral auth/client code, token stores, webhook capture/normalization, candidate/session aggregation, Call Log sync, Analytics reconciliation, idempotency, duplicate detection, and shadow/real call-lead ingest.
- `ringcentral-config.ts` is the single source for RingCentral env toggles, collection names, collection mode, duplicate window, cron lookback/overlap, and lead write posture.
- `call-lead-sources.ts` owns mapped inbound numbers and source-company attribution. Do not duplicate those mappings in routes, scripts, or tests.
- `ringcentral-call-lead-ingest.service.ts` is the shared promotion path for webhook and Call Log qualified calls. Do not create RingCentral `call_leads` directly from route, webhook, cron, or script code.

## Admin V1 API Surface

- Admin routes are mounted in `api/routes/v1.routes.ts` under `/api/v1/admin/...` and remain protected by `requireApiSecret` like the public v1 surface.
- Admin read services live in `api/services/admin/`:
  - `adminScope.service.ts` selects production runtime models or read-only historical models from `scripts/historical/models/`.
  - `adminBrowse.service.ts` owns paginated browse, detail lookup, common filters, sorting, and export row retrieval.
  - `adminSearch.service.ts` owns grouped global search across form leads, call leads, bookings, cancellations, customers, and agents.
  - `adminExport.service.ts` owns CSV column selection and export response payload generation.
- Admin query schemas live in `api/validation/v1/admin.validation.ts` and are re-exported by `api/validation/v1.validation.ts`.
- Implemented admin GET endpoints:
  - `/api/v1/admin/form-leads` and `/api/v1/admin/form-leads/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/call-leads` and `/api/v1/admin/call-leads/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/booked-leads` and `/api/v1/admin/booked-leads/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/cancelled-leads` and `/api/v1/admin/cancelled-leads/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/customers` and `/api/v1/admin/customers/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/agents` and `/api/v1/admin/agents/:id`
  - `/api/v1/admin/search`
  - `/api/v1/admin/exports/form-leads.csv`, `/call-leads.csv`, `/booked-leads.csv`, `/cancelled-leads.csv`, `/customers.csv`, and `/agents.csv`
- Admin browse responses use `{ ok: true, data: { items, page, limit, total, has_next_page } }`; detail responses use `{ ok: true, data }`; CSV exports return `text/csv` with an attachment filename.
- `database_scope=production|historical|combined` is supported where appropriate. Detail endpoints reject `combined`; historical scope is read-only; combined is for search/export-style reads.
- Do not add `/api/v1/admin` delete routes. Admin write workflows should continue to use existing public v1 booking, cancellation, lead, and customer write services so Google Sheet sync behavior is preserved.

## Observability Surface

- `api/services/observability/` owns operational events, incidents, notification policy, digest/retry behavior, admin overview reads, and operational report generation.
- Runtime Mongoose schemas for this surface live in `api/models/OperationalEvent.ts`, `OperationalIncident.ts`, `NotificationDelivery.ts`, `OperationalReportRun.ts`, and must be accessed through `observabilityModelFactory.ts` so collection modes stay respected.
- Observability config lives in `api/config/domain/observability.ts`; avoid direct `process.env` reads for observability policy outside that file unless the value is unrelated route auth like `CRON_SECRET`.
- Notification digest cron is mounted from `api/routes/notification-cron.routes.ts`; it should remain a thin authenticated boundary around observability services.
- Rollup collections are deferred for now. Admin overview and reports aggregate directly from operational event, incident, notification delivery, and report-run collections.

## Script Launch Map

- Run scripts through `package.json` commands so `.env` loading and `tsx` invocation remain consistent.
- `scripts/dev_ops/` is for personal/admin DB operations and seed-style utilities. Treat these as potentially destructive even when names sound local.
- `scripts/historical/` owns historical backfill, reconciliation, diagnostics, analytics, and repairs. `scripts/historical/models/` contains relaxed replicas for the separate `vantagemovershistorical` DB, not runtime API models.
- `scripts/google_sheets/` owns sheet inspection, diagnostics, auth helpers, structure copy, and service-account utilities.
- `scripts/postman/` owns Postman login and collection/environment sync for API tooling.
- `scripts/ringcentral/` owns manual diagnostics, webhook subscription management, Call Log validation/probes, workflow tests, and manual cron/reconcile runners. Keep reusable runtime logic in `api/services/ringcentral/` and call it from scripts.
- `scripts/google_apps_scripts/` owns Google Apps Script form helpers; `scripts/demos/` owns runnable demos/fixtures; `scripts/docs/` contains generated source-workbook documentation.

## Working Rules

- Preserve live workflow boundaries: form leads, call leads, bookings, cancellations, customers, CRM submission, Google Sheets sync, reconciliation, enrichment, historical backfill, and Postman collection management are separate concerns even when they share models.
- Prefer adding focused modules inside the existing domain folders over expanding broad orchestration files.
- Keep side effects explicit: Mongo is the source of truth; CRM, Google Sheets, Postman, and historical workbooks are external targets that need mode-aware logging and safe identifiers.
- Use `pnpm test` for `api/**/*.test.ts` and `pnpm typecheck` after runtime TypeScript changes when practical.
